.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_FREE_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_free_entry \- free an entry back to the pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool_free_entry(mp, entry);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns an allocated entry to the pool's free list. Macro wrapper around
_ccol_mempool_free_entry() that sets pointer to NULL.

The pool is named by the caller rather than derived from the entry. Free
an entry to the pool it was allocated from; passing a different pool is a
caller error and is fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp     Pool the entry was allocated from
entry  Entry to free (will be set to NULL after freeing)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) complexity
Safe to call with a NULL entry; a NULL pool is a caller error and fatal
Performs corruption detection via assertions

On a thread-safe pool the entry is parked in a small per-thread cache,
and the shared free list is reached only when that cache is full. A pool
too small to maintain one, meaning fewer than 8 elements, takes the
pool's lock on every call instead. See
ccol_mempool_create(3) for how to size a pool.

Corruption and double-free detection are unaffected by caching. An entry
sitting in a cache is still marked free, so freeing it a second time is
detected exactly as it would be otherwise. Two threads freeing the same
entry at the same instant are the exception: the check that catches it
runs outside the lock, so one of the two can miss the other's mark. That
is a data race in the calling program either way, and it is the same
limit a thread-caching general purpose allocator has.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Concurrent callers do not contend on the common path. An entry freed by
one thread may be handed out to another.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_alloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_free_entry (3)
